.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-SCALE-APPLICATION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-scale-application \- change how many instances an application runs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap scale-application
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-instances\fR|\fB\-i\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-min\-instances\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-max\-instances\fR \fIN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Changes how many instances an application runs, without restarting the ones
it already has.
.PP
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1)
can set the same two fields, but it writes the whole definition and stamps
the modification date \- and the manager restarts a pool whose application
has changed since it started it. Scaling that way therefore stops every
running instance and starts it again, which is the opposite of what asking
for more capacity means, and worst at the moment somebody asks for it.
.PP
What is set is the range the autoscaler works within, not a count. The
manager picks the change up on its next reconcile, within a few seconds,
and scales toward it \- adding instances one at a time and stopping idle
ones as the load allows. Nothing is started or stopped by this command
itself.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-application\-id ", " \-n " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application to scale, in the namespace the session works in.
.TP
.BR \-\-instances ", " \-i " " \fIN\fR
Run exactly this many: shorthand for setting both bounds to the same number.
That pins the pool at that size and leaves the autoscaler nothing to decide,
which is useful to hold an application steady and worth undoing afterwards.
Cannot be combined with the two below.
.TP
.B \-\-min\-instances " " \fIN\fR
Smallest number of instances to keep running. Left as it stands if not
given, so a ceiling can be raised without touching the floor.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-instances " " \fIN\fR
Largest number the autoscaler may run. Left as it stands if not given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Non-zero if the application does not exist, if nothing was named to change,
or if the bounds would leave a floor above a ceiling. That last check is
made against the bound as it
.I will
stand rather than as it is, so raising only the floor is refused when it
would pass the stored ceiling.
.PP
A floor of zero is refused. An application desired RUNNING with no instances
reads in every listing as a pool that has failed to start;
.BR euclid-cli-eap-stop-application (1)
is how one is taken out of service in a way that says so.
.SH EXAMPLES
Give an application more room to scale under load:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap scale-application --application-id transformation --max-instances 16
.fi
.RE
.PP
Guarantee four instances are always up:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap scale-application --application-id parser --min-instances 4
.fi
.RE
.PP
Hold it at exactly two, whatever the load:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap scale-application --application-id splitting --instances 2
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-stop-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-get-application (1)
